WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.934
-Jimmy!

00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:06.934
-That is exactly what --
That's the perfect segue

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:09.567
into my first question,
you celebrating like that.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:12.601
I have a picture that I saw of
you at the U.S. Open.

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:13.601
-Oh, yes.

00:00:13.601 --> 00:00:15.634
-Look at you celebrating
for Serena.

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:19.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:21.067
Is that a real celebration

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:22.367
or is that just a one-up
for Instagram?

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.534
-Oh, no, that's like
the realest --

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:27.801
That's like the most Serena...
-Really?

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:29.367
-...expression ever.

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.601
Like, when I watch her, even on
the couch, I'm like that.

00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:32.667
-Really?
-Yeah, it's crazy.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:33.834
-You're like, "Yeah, come on!"
-Yeah.

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:35.701
I mean, also,
you know what helps?

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:37.334
You know the U.S. Open drinks?

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:40.901
The melon ones with the little
tennis ball melons?

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:42.267
-A couple of those?

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:43.767
-Yeah, 'cause when I get there,
I'm a wreck.

00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:45.167
Like, I'm so anxious.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.400
-Because you're friends
with Serena.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.567
-Yeah, so, I, like, take it
really personally.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:51.901
I think that she can really feel
that I'm there supporting her.

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:54.000
She has no idea.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.734
-No, but she does --
-Until after the game,

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:57.667
she doesn't really know
I'm there, probably.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.434
-You're very loud.
-Very loud.

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:01.701
-Yeah, you're a great friend
to have.

00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:03.834
So, this last match
you're watching,

00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:05.200
you're just like,
"Come on, bud."

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.367
-Yeah. I mean, no, just, like,
when -- when --

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.767
you know, if she was serving
one way or --

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.868
-You know.
-And then it just happens,

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:17.601
and I can feel her feel it,
then, oh, it's just the best.

00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:20.133
-You were saying you text her
and she'll get back to you,

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:23.400
like, during --
-Oh, yeah, during the game.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.000
She gets back to me
during the game.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.367
No, no, no. I just try to send
like a supportive message.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.534
And you're like,
"She's not going to respond.

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:31.400
Like, "She's in the U.S. Open
this week."

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.534
-Well, what do you do? If you
hang out with Serena Williams,

00:01:33.534 --> 00:01:35.834
what would you do with Serena?

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:38.434
-Karaoke, mostly.
-Really?

00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:41.634
-Serena is a karaoke,
like, legend.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:42.868
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:44.601
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I love knowing this.

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:46.334
What is her go-to?
Do you know what her song is?

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:47.767
-I can't.
You'll have to ask her.

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.200
She's on next week, right?
-She's coming on.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.300
-You'll have to ask her
what her go-to song is.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.267
-What is yours?

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.000
-Mine goes a lot of
different directions.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.234
Really depends on the night.

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:58.033
I try to take it --
keep the vibes going.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:00.834
But sometimes I'll throw in like
a Disney song here and there.

00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:02.367
Swear to God.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:03.734
-Yeah. I can see you doing that.

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:05.634
-Like, "I just can't wait
to be king."

00:02:05.634 --> 00:02:09.334
-That's your jam?
"Lion King" is your jam?

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:10.868
-Sometimes I'll throw it
in there, yeah.

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:12.734
If I'm feeling it.
-I don't know if I say this

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:15.000
every time you're on the show,
I love your voice.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.734
-You do?
-Yeah. I think you should do

00:02:16.734 --> 00:02:19.801
a movie, or an animated movie,
or a podcast,

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:21.033
or something like that.
-Thank you.

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.334
-Yeah. I just love it.

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:23.868
I'm always like,
"Oh, what a great voice."

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:25.567
-Oh, I appreciate that.
I feel like you're probably

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:26.968
one of the only people
that thinks that.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:28.200
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.601
-Oh, no way, no way.
-Thanks, Jimmy.

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:32.534
But it means the most to me.
Like, obviously -- you know.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:33.734
-I hear a lot of voices.

00:02:33.734 --> 00:02:35.334
-You have one of the best voices
of all time.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.367
-[ Laughs ] I don't, but --

00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:40.234
-He does, he does, right?

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:41.767
[ Cheers and applause ]
-No.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.434
So, here's why -- one of
the reasons why I love you.

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:45.767
Last time I was talking to you,
you said,

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.868
"I'm working on
this clothing brand."

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:49.567
-Mm-hmm.
-And I go, "Oh, yeah.

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.367
You should do this."
Like, Gigi Hadid just come out

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:52.567
with your --"
and you go, "I know,

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.000
but I'm actually doing it
the right way.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.400
I want to make sure
it's perfect."

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.300
This was years ago.
-Thanks.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.434
-I mean, a lot work
goes into this stuff.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:04.334
-I mean, you know, I think that
it was the next natural step

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.501
for me was to start
a clothing company.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:10.701
And I felt a lot of pressure
for a few years to do that.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:14.200
Whether that be from a deal or,
you know, people on my team.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.601
And I was like, "Look, I'm just
going to take my time and --

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:19.000
I don't need to do a fast
fashion company just because,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.200
you know, the offer is there
and whatever."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.567
So, I want to the take my time
and look at my wardrobe

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:26.868
and think about something that
would feel genuine for me

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:30.400
to make and also for people to
see that coming from me and know

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.133
that it does come from something
that I care about.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:37.200
And cashmere -- Yeah.
-You're wearing it tonight.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.367
-I'm wearing it tonight.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.501
This is the coziest
I've ever been on TV.

00:03:40.501 --> 00:03:42.033
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-So, this is great.

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:43.100
-You really are.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.300
You were saying it's called --

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.267
Guest in Residence is the label.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.968
What's the meaning behind
the name Guest in Residence?

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:55.234
-Guest in Residence.
Well, to me, Guest in Residence

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.467
is someone like Jimmy,

00:03:57.467 --> 00:04:00.934
who walks into every room with
a warmth and a sense of comfort

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:03.400
in themselves that they're ready

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.934
to take on the world
around them,

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.267
have wonder about the person
in front of them.

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:10.634
And that's just like
a cozy person to me.

00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:13.133
So it kind of just felt like
this material

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:16.133
that could go do those things
with people.

00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:20.334
And this, you know, has such
a sustainable quality to it,

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:24.334
this material, that I really
want to encourage

00:04:24.334 --> 00:04:28.734
this generation to invest in
good quality pieces

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.033
and keep them for a long time
in their wardrobes

00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:33.234
and then pass them down
to people they love.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.734
And, yeah, you know,
it is a luxury item.

00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:38.234
I don't need people to buy
10 sweaters a year.

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:41.400
But I want to encourage thinking
about what you're consuming.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.300
-You were saying what inspired
you, one of the reasons that --

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.567
Was it your mom bought you
a sweater

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.000
when you first moved to
the city?

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.767
-Yeah, yeah.
When I came to New York,

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:57.133
it was a crazy winter
my first year of college.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:59.133
It was, like, the coldest winter
ever.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:02.033
There was one winter,
the snow was piled up

00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.300
on both sides of the street.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.501
Like, you couldn't even cross
the street.

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:05.567
-You couldn't even see
the trash bags.

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.200
-You couldn't even --
Yeah, exactly.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.467
So, I took a sweater

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:12.067
from each of my parents
for this,

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:13.701
you know, first big winter of
mine.

00:05:13.701 --> 00:05:16.067
And it kind of was this piece
that I would, you know,

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:18.133
one, you never get rid of it
because it means something.

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.734
It kind of becomes this heirloom
that you learn to take care of

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:24.734
and that has this really nice,
nostalgic feeling to it.

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:27.133
And I just felt --
-Yeah.

00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:30.501
So, everyone on your Christmas
list, get Guest in Residence,

00:05:30.501 --> 00:05:32.767
and get one special one
and just hand it down

00:05:32.767 --> 00:05:34.467
for years and years to come.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:37.701
It is the softest, by the way.

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:39.167
-Oh, Jimmy!

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:43.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.567
-Ooh, I love it.

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:46.834
-I'm wearing the socks.
-Oh, the socks as well.

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:49.601
We got it all going down.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:51.100
Let me throw out
a pair of socks.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.067
You're going to love
one of these.

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:54.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:56.767
Guest in Residence. Split them.

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.968
Hand them -- One wears one sock.
One wears the other.

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:00.901
-I'm throwing some.
-You got one?

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:02.534
We're throwing out socks,
people.

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:04.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.734
We're throwing out socks.

00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:09.334
That is Guest in Residence
right there.

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:13.033
Ladies and gentlemen,
Gigi Hadid!

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:14.467
Guest in Residence.

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:16.834
We'll be right back
with Blake Shelton, everybody.

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:17.968
Come on back!

